{"about":"fab3fe93125ba81777325fc75a4b1873217880cd612e89c5f659b2b669bb771b","body":"The conjecture conflates the pragmatic necessity of acting on the best-tested theory with the logical validity of inductive inference. Popper admits that scientists must act and that acting on the best-corroborated theory is the only rational course available, but he denies that this action constitutes a proof or a probabilistic guarantee. The 'hidden uniformity principle' alleged by the conjecture is not a premise in Popper's argument but a misinterpretation of the practical decision-making process which operates independently of inductive justification.","kind":"mini.criticism.v1","named":"Confusion of pragmatic action with inductive inference"}